referenceSolutions.java:
class Solution {
  public int magnitudeOrderFloorTicket(int[] readings, int k) {
    int[] a=readings; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {Integer[]copy=Arrays.stream(a).boxed().toArray(Integer[]::new);Arrays.sort(copy,Comparator.comparingInt((Integer x)->Math.abs(x)).thenComparingInt(x->x));out.addAll(Arrays.asList(copy));}
    int answer=out.isEmpty()?0:out.get(0);for(int x:out)answer=Math.min(answer,x);return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
The working idea

Sort readings by absolute value ascending, breaking ties by numeric value ascending. Keep duplicate readings.

Return the smallest value in the derived sequence, or 0 for an empty sequence.

Why this works

Each derived value follows the unit's stated order and includes exactly the items allowed by its definition. The receipt then folds or filters those values without changing their meaning. Check the empty, singleton, repeated and maximum-size inputs before trusting the final total.

Target time O(n log n); space O(n).

